3. Dangerous Lexica and Dataset
3.1. Dangerous Language
We deﬁne dangerous language as a statement of an inten-
tion to inﬂict physical pain, injury, or damage on someone
in retribution for something done or not. This deﬁnition ex-
cludes threats that do not reﬂect physical harm on the side
of the receiver end of the threat. The deﬁnition also ex-
cludes tongue in cheek whose real intention is to tease. An
example of this later category is a threat made in the con-
text of sports where it is common among fans to tease one
another using metaphorical, string language (see Example
# 6 in Section 3.3.).
